{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Why have you come to me, seeing you hated me and sent me away from you.</b> He mentioned to them three matters. The first: 'Why have you come to me' — to Be'er-Sheva, where I am now. The second: 'And you hated me' — because of my success and the abundance of flocks that I possess. The third: 'And you sent me away from you' — that is, from Gerar, where I had been settling, for you said to me, 'Go from among us.'" "<b>They answered</b> all three. You say, 'Why have you come to me?' — 'We saw plainly that HaShem was with you,' that is, we saw in the sowing, we saw in the wells, in all your deeds we saw your success. And it says, 'Let there now be an oath between us, between us and you.' The Targum explains: 'the hatred that was between us shall be annulled.' <b>But</b> R. Yosef interpreted the words in another sense, as follows: 'Let there now be an oath between us' — that we not harm one another, and this matter shall be hidden between us and you, so that the world shall not know that the king imposes an oath upon a man not to harm him, and fear of him has fallen upon him. And as for what you say, 'And you hated me' — far be it, if you do us harm, etc. And as for what you say, 'And you sent me away from you' — 'We will send you away in peace'; if so, what grievances do you have against us? Should you say, perhaps in the land of Gerar you prospered but not so in other lands and other places — you are now blessed of HaShem; behold, you are in Be'er-Sheva blessed of HaShem, and in every place success comes to meet you."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:25.038917722Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}